  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how metal species can be dispersed or confined on different solid supports, emphasizing a progression from single atoms to larger mixed-metal particles. At the top, a metal surface is shown hosting increasingly complex species: an isolated atom, a small cluster, a bimetallic cluster, a “single-atom alloy” nanoparticle, and finally a larger bimetallic nanoparticle. This conveys how metals can exist in several structural forms on a substrate depending on aggregation and composition.\n\nThe lower panels compare porous supports that restrict where metals can sit. One schematic shows a material with straight and sinusoidal channels; single atoms and clusters occupy the channels, while a larger metal cluster is trapped in a larger intersectional void. This highlights size-selective confinement inside pore networks. The other schematic depicts a framework built from inorganic nodes connected by organic linkers, resembling a metal–organic framework. Here, metal species can attach either to inorganic nodes or to organic linkers, while a larger metal cluster is confined inside an internal cavity. Overall, the figure illustrates different ways supports stabilize isolated atoms, clusters, and nanoparticles through surface anchoring and pore confinement.",
